1
00:00:03,090 --> 00:00:40,650
The virtue. We all learned that when we were young. It will come into play during our filtrations in this video.

2
00:00:40,650 --> 00:00:48,650
The process of filtration is used to separate your solid product from solvent, or to remove impurities from your solution.

3
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:59,650
This video will discuss three types of filtration. Microscale filtration via a pasture pipette, gravity filtration, and finally vacuum filtration.

4
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:10,270
First, let's address microscale filtration. We'll be using the pasture pipette to carry out this type of filtration.

5
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:14,270
I bet you never thought you could have this much fun on such a small scale.

6
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:35,600
Here's how to prepare your filter pipette. Here's a piece of cotton. Break off a small bit. Roll this into a small ball.

7
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:48,060
The ball should be about the size of the top opening of the pipette. Place the cotton ball there.

8
00:01:48,060 --> 00:02:01,490
You can use either copper wire or another pipette to stuff down the cotton. With a few jabs, it should be nicely wedged in there.

9
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:10,990
You want to firmly pack it down so it enjoys a snug fit between the wide portion and the narrow portion of the pipette.

10
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:14,990
But let's add something extra.

11
00:02:14,990 --> 00:02:19,990
As you can see, here is the cotton. But sitting on top of it, we've placed sea light.

12
00:02:19,990 --> 00:02:28,990
What is it, you may ask? Sea light is a very fine powder used to filter very small particles. We'll be using it in this filtration.

13
00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:41,010
Now for the setup. Here we have the mixture to be filtered. And here we have our filter pipette, which is securely clamped to a stand.

14
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:45,010
And we've placed a collection vial underneath it.

15
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:59,300
Now it's time for filtration. Draw up some of the mixture with another pipette. Transfer the mixture to your filter pipette.

16
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:19,550
Allow it to do its filtering job under the tip of the filter pipette. Can you see? Squint if you must.

17
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:23,550
The filtered liquid should be draining out into your collection vial.

18
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:35,880
Alright, alright, I'll spare you the squinting. Look, our once black mixture is now clear when filtered.

19
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:41,880
But what's that I hear? You're a bulb fiend? Or what's worse, you just have no patience?

20
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:48,880
If you insist on using the bulb to expedite your filtration, then there are some rules for its usage.

21
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:58,880
When using the bulb, don't push so eagerly that impurities get pushed through the cotton or that the cotton is pushed out itself.

22
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:08,880
And once you begin, never release the bulb since air, impurities, or even the cotton could be sucked back up.

23
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:12,880
Let's get back on track and take care of rinsing.

24
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:17,880
As you can see here, there's residual mixture left in the flask.

25
00:04:17,880 --> 00:04:24,880
Rinse the flask with solvent, allowing it to run down the sides and collect any residual mixture.

26
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:35,880
Draw up these washings into the pipette and transfer them, adding the washings to the filter pipette, allowing them to be filtered themselves.

27
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:41,880
You need to rinse the flask and add its washings a total of three times.

28
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:46,880
When the filtration's all done, we'll have the filtered product.

29
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:50,880
Here it is, the beauty free of impurities.

30
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:54,880
But let's take one last look at our filter pipette.

31
00:04:54,880 --> 00:05:02,880
Here it is after the filtration. As you can see, impurities have collected on top of the sea light.

32
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:08,880
So that was microscale. Let's go macroscale with gravity filtration.

33
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:13,880
First, let's explore chemistry origami while fluting our filter paper.

34
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:16,880
Here's our goal.

35
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,880
You should always fold when fluting.

36
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:23,880
Making a harsh crease might cause filter paper to tear eventually.

37
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:25,880
Let's get started.

38
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:28,880
First, you want to fold it in half.

39
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:38,310
Open it up and fold it in half once again, this time in the opposite direction.

40
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:46,790
You want to pinch the bottom and push in, like so.

41
00:05:46,790 --> 00:05:49,790
Notice how it now forms a sort of X.

42
00:05:49,790 --> 00:06:03,190
Open it slightly and do the pinch and push move once again, this time in the opposite direction.

43
00:06:03,190 --> 00:06:06,190
As you can see, you're already halfway there.

44
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:10,950
You should have four main sections.

45
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:14,950
Take one section and separate it from the rest.

46
00:06:14,950 --> 00:06:19,950
Push in the crease and fold back the little triangles that form.

47
00:06:19,950 --> 00:06:24,950
You want to do the same thing for the next three sections.

48
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:29,950
Essentially, you're forming two little triangles from one big one.

49
00:06:29,950 --> 00:06:33,950
Like a mother giving birth to twins, isn't that great?

50
00:06:33,950 --> 00:06:40,470
You're halfway to a fully fluted filter paper.

51
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:43,470
Try saying that three times fast.

52
00:06:43,470 --> 00:07:07,210
By the end of this, all the folds should be alternating.

53
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:10,210
This creates the fluted majesty.

54
00:07:10,210 --> 00:07:16,610
Don't worry, you'll get the hang of it.

55
00:07:16,610 --> 00:07:21,620
You may be asking, why should we even bother to flute?

56
00:07:21,620 --> 00:07:27,620
Well, it increases the surface area of contact between the mixture and the filter paper.

57
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:31,620
Therefore, a more efficient filtration.

58
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:33,620
Now for the setup.

59
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:40,770
Here we have our mixture to be filtered, and here we have our collection flask.

60
00:07:40,770 --> 00:07:45,460
Into the flask, place your funnel.

61
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:48,460
It should be appropriately sized.

62
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:52,460
And into the funnel, place the fluted filter paper.

63
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:55,460
Don't you feel proud?

64
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:59,460
Now that we're all set up, it's time for filtration.

65
00:07:59,460 --> 00:08:05,180
Pour the mixture onto the filter paper.

66
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:09,180
Be sure not to pour too much that it overflows.

67
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:15,680
As the filtration progresses, add more of the mixture.

68
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:43,780
But remember, there's always rinsing.

69
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:47,780
Here we can see the residual mixture in the flask.

70
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:52,780
Using a pipette, make sure to rinse your flask with solvent,

71
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:57,780
allowing it to run down the sides and pick up the residual mixture.

72
00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:00,780
Then add these washings to the filter paper.

73
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:10,130
Ah, but don't forget, you want to rinse the filter paper with solvent as well.

74
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:13,790
Use a pipette.

75
00:09:13,790 --> 00:09:19,790
Like always, you want to rinse your flask and the filter paper three times each.

76
00:09:19,790 --> 00:09:25,790
When you think gravity's done all that it can, it's time for collecting the product.

77
00:09:25,790 --> 00:09:30,790
If you were aiming for the solid product, take the filter paper out of the funnel

78
00:09:30,790 --> 00:09:34,790
and place it on another piece of filter paper or a watch glass

79
00:09:34,790 --> 00:09:37,790
and allow the product to dry overnight.

80
00:09:37,790 --> 00:09:44,100
But what if you have three p-sets, a test tomorrow, a paper due the next day,

81
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:49,100
and your mixture just doesn't seem to understand you're pressed for time?

82
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:53,100
Well, then you have the option of vacuum filtration.

83
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:59,090
For vacuum filtration, you can use either the Buechner or the Hirsch funnel,

84
00:09:59,090 --> 00:10:03,090
depending on what you're filtering and how much there is to filter.

85
00:10:03,090 --> 00:10:10,580
Really, there's not much difference between the two besides shape and size.

86
00:10:10,580 --> 00:10:14,430
Now for the setup.

87
00:10:14,430 --> 00:10:18,430
Here we have our filter flask. Notice its characteristic side arm.

88
00:10:18,430 --> 00:10:21,430
Make sure the flask is clamped firmly to a stand.

89
00:10:21,430 --> 00:10:25,430
When it's all put together, the filter and funnel will make a top-heavy structure,

90
00:10:25,430 --> 00:10:27,430
making it prone to fall.

91
00:10:27,430 --> 00:10:30,430
So a clamp is your best bet.

92
00:10:30,430 --> 00:10:32,430
And here is our Buechner funnel.

93
00:10:32,430 --> 00:10:36,430
If it doesn't already have a rubber adapter, add one.

94
00:10:36,430 --> 00:10:44,450
Securely fit the funnel and adapter into the top part of the flask.

95
00:10:44,450 --> 00:10:46,450
But what's that you say?

96
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:49,450
The fit between the funnel and the flask isn't snug enough?

97
00:10:49,450 --> 00:10:51,450
Well, there's an alternative.

98
00:10:51,450 --> 00:10:57,300
Another type of rubber adapter.

99
00:10:57,300 --> 00:10:59,300
Notice its shape and size.

100
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:02,300
And look in the middle there.

101
00:11:02,300 --> 00:11:06,580
There's a raised area.

102
00:11:06,580 --> 00:11:10,580
This raised area fits into the mouth of the flask, as seen here.

103
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:13,580
Push down so that the flat side is on the top,

104
00:11:13,580 --> 00:11:17,580
and there's a nice snug fit at the top of the mouth.

105
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:22,580
Then take your Buechner funnel and securely fit it into the adapter.

106
00:11:22,580 --> 00:11:27,240
Regardless of the type of adapter you use,

107
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:31,240
be sure to have a snug fit before you proceed with your filtration.

108
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:38,060
The piece of filter paper should be large enough to cover the funnel's base,

109
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:40,060
but not fold up onto the sides.

110
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:43,060
Now add the water trap to the setup.

111
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:46,060
Make sure it's firmly clamped to the stand.

112
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:49,060
It consists of a filter flask.

113
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:53,590
Also, notice its sidearm.

114
00:11:53,590 --> 00:11:55,590
A rubber stopper.

115
00:11:55,590 --> 00:11:58,590
This can also be some sort of glass stopper.

116
00:11:58,590 --> 00:12:08,400
And tubing.

117
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:12,400
Attach one end of vacuum tubing to the tube on the water trap,

118
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:15,400
and the other end to the sidearm of the filter flask.

119
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:20,400
Then attach the sidearm of the water trap to the water aspirator

120
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:22,400
via more vacuum tubing.

121
00:12:22,400 --> 00:12:25,870
So at this point, you might be wondering,

122
00:12:25,870 --> 00:12:29,870
what do the water aspirator and water trap do anyway?

123
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:32,870
When the water aspirator is turned on,

124
00:12:32,870 --> 00:12:34,870
out comes a rush of water.

125
00:12:34,870 --> 00:12:38,870
This rush of water causes suction in the tubing.

126
00:12:38,870 --> 00:12:42,870
This then suctions out the air from the system.

127
00:12:42,870 --> 00:12:47,870
Consequently, a vacuum is created in both the filter flask and the water trap.

128
00:12:47,870 --> 00:12:49,870
Now we can filter.

129
00:12:49,870 --> 00:12:55,870
When we finally finish, we'll have to turn off the aspirator.

130
00:12:55,870 --> 00:12:59,870
This may cause water to rush back into our system,

131
00:12:59,870 --> 00:13:01,870
and this could be potentially bad.

132
00:13:01,870 --> 00:13:07,870
But look, our water trap prevents the water from entering our filter flask.

133
00:13:07,870 --> 00:13:12,870
And so luckily, our filtrate is saved from any water contamination.

134
00:13:12,870 --> 00:13:19,500
Yay!

135
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:26,850
This is why we love, and more importantly, use, the water trap.

136
00:13:26,850 --> 00:13:30,850
But what if you're using the house vacuum line instead of the water aspirator?

137
00:13:30,850 --> 00:13:34,850
Well, the filter flask sidearm should then be directly attached

138
00:13:34,850 --> 00:13:37,850
to the house vacuum nozzle via tubing.

139
00:13:37,850 --> 00:13:41,850
Don't worry about any intermediaries like the water trap.

140
00:13:41,850 --> 00:13:46,850
So now it's time for filtration.

141
00:13:46,850 --> 00:13:50,850
Turn on the water aspirator at full force.

142
00:13:50,850 --> 00:13:53,850
The vacuum should suck down the filter paper.

143
00:13:53,850 --> 00:13:57,850
Wet the filter paper with solvent so that it sticks to the base of the funnel.

144
00:13:57,850 --> 00:14:00,850
You don't want it floating up later.

145
00:14:00,850 --> 00:14:03,850
Here's our mixture to be filtered.

146
00:14:03,850 --> 00:14:08,130
Take the mixture and pour it into the funnel.

147
00:14:08,130 --> 00:14:13,130
But make sure that you pour the mixture into the middle of the funnel.

148
00:14:13,130 --> 00:14:22,210
Don't pour it in all at once.

149
00:14:22,210 --> 00:14:26,210
Pour in more of the mixture as the filtration progresses.

150
00:14:26,210 --> 00:14:36,730
We can't forget that rinsing, though.

151
00:14:36,730 --> 00:14:41,200
As usual, there's residual mixture left.

152
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:43,200
Rinse the flask with solvent,

153
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:51,910
allowing it to run down the sides and pick up any residual mixture.

154
00:14:51,910 --> 00:14:59,680
And add these washings to the filter flask.

155
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:04,680
And remember to rinse the filter paper with solvent as well.

156
00:15:04,680 --> 00:15:07,680
Round and round goes the pipette.

157
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:14,680
You should rinse your flask and the filter paper in the funnel three times each.

158
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:17,680
Allow the filtration to come to an end.

159
00:15:17,680 --> 00:15:26,590
Once you think it has, then it's time for turning off the aspirator.

160
00:15:26,590 --> 00:15:28,590
If you did not attach a water trap,

161
00:15:28,590 --> 00:15:31,590
take tubing off before turning off the aspirator.

162
00:15:31,590 --> 00:15:35,590
Otherwise, water might rush into your flask.

163
00:15:35,590 --> 00:15:39,590
So how about collecting the product?

164
00:15:39,590 --> 00:15:41,590
If you were aiming for the solid product,

165
00:15:41,590 --> 00:15:48,590
use a spatula or some sort of tool to release the filter paper from the funnel.

166
00:15:48,590 --> 00:15:52,590
Set it down on another piece of filter paper or a watch glass

167
00:15:52,590 --> 00:15:56,590
and allow the product to dry overnight.

168
00:15:56,590 --> 00:15:59,590
So now you're finally all filtered.

169
00:15:59,590 --> 00:16:03,590
Now that you've got the skills, go out and purify the world.

170
00:16:03,590 --> 00:16:10,630
Remember, this video is intended to help you prepare for lab

171
00:16:10,630 --> 00:16:14,630
by providing a demonstration of the proper experimental technique.

172
00:16:14,630 --> 00:16:18,630
It is not intended as a replacement for reading your lab manual

173
00:16:18,630 --> 00:16:20,630
or the supplementary material.

174
00:16:20,630 --> 00:16:23,630
In order to become a great experimentalist,

175
00:16:23,630 --> 00:16:27,630
it is important that you understand both theory and technique.

176
00:16:27,630 --> 00:16:29,630
Now it's your turn. Good luck.
